Welch Degrees of Freedom

Degrees of freedom note
In this course, we are not testing your ability to be a calculator in an exam setting.
For hand calculations, you may use the conservative degrees of freedom:
\[
df = \min(n_1 - 1,\; n_2 - 1)
\]


A more accurate approximation, commonly used by software, is the Welch–Satterthwaite degrees of freedom:
\[
df =
\frac{
\left(\frac{s_1^2}{n_1}+\frac{s_2^2}{n_2}\right)^2
}{
\frac{\left(s_1^2/n_1\right)^2}{n_1-1}
+
\frac{\left(s_2^2/n_2\right)^2}{n_2-1}
}
\]


For exams in this course, use the conservative version unless told otherwise.

5.4.2 Difference of Proportions

For two independent population proportions:
\[
(\hat p_1-\hat p_2)\pm z_{\alpha/2}
\sqrt{
\frac{\hat p_1(1-\hat p_1)}{n_1}
+
\frac{\hat p_2(1-\hat p_2)}{n_2}
}
\]


Check large counts in both groups: successes and failures should each be at least 10.

6.3.2 Difference of Proportions

When testing \(H_0:p_1-p_2=0\), pool the sample proportions under the null:
\[
\hat p=\frac{x_1+x_2}{n_1+n_2}
\]
\[
z^*=\frac{\hat p_1-\hat p_2}
{\sqrt{\hat p(1-\hat p)(1/n_1+1/n_2)}}
\]

Difference of Means: Known \(\sigma_1\) and \(\sigma_2\)

When the population standard deviations are known, a level \(100(1-\alpha)\%\) confidence interval for \(\mu_1-\mu_2\) is:
\[
(\bar{x}_1-\bar{x}_2)
\pm
z_{1-\alpha/2}
\sqrt{
\frac{\sigma_1^2}{n_1}
+
\frac{\sigma_2^2}{n_2}
}
\]


This case is mathematically useful but rare in real applications, because population standard deviations are usually unknown.

5.4.2 Confidence Interval for a Difference of Proportions

For two independent samples, let:
\[
\hat{p}_1=\frac{x_1}{n_1},
\qquad
\hat{p}_2=\frac{x_2}{n_2}
\]
A level \(100(1-\alpha)\%\) confidence interval for \(p_1-p_2\) is:
\[
(\hat{p}_1-\hat{p}_2)
\pm
z_{1-\alpha/2}
\sqrt{
\frac{\hat{p}_1(1-\hat{p}_1)}{n_1}
+
\frac{\hat{p}_2(1-\hat{p}_2)}{n_2}
}
\]


The interval estimates the difference between two population proportions, not the difference between two sample counts.

Multinomial Counts

If the model probabilities are \(p_1,\ldots,p_k\), the expected count in category \(i\) is:
\[
E_i=np_i
\]
The Pearson chi-square statistic is:
\[
Q_{k-1}
=
\sum_{i=1}^{k}
\frac{(Y_i-np_i)^2}{np_i}
=
\sum_{i=1}^{k}
\frac{(O_i-E_i)^2}{E_i}
\]
For large enough expected counts,
\[
Q_{k-1}\approx \chi^2_{k-1}
\]


The statistic gets large when observed counts are far from expected counts.

10.2 Chi-square Test for Goodness of Fit

A chi-square goodness-of-fit test checks whether observed category counts agree with a hypothesized probability model.
The hypotheses are:
\[
H_0:
p_1=p_{hyp,1},\;
p_2=p_{hyp,2},\;
\ldots,\;
p_k=p_{hyp,k}
\]
\[
H_a:\text{at least one category probability is different}
\]


The null hypothesis can say “all categories are equal,” but it can also give unequal hypothesized probabilities.
